# ── line endings ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# This is not cosmetic here, it is a CORRECTNESS setting.
#
# `hud/` is the upstream plugin shipped byte for byte, and `npm run check:verbatim`
# proves it by comparing sha256 hashes. With `core.autocrlf=true` (the Windows
# default in many installs) git rewrites every LF to CRLF on checkout, so a fresh
# clone FAILS the check with every vendored file reported as modified — and
# `npm run verify`, which is the release gate, fails with it.
#
# `eol=lf` pins the working tree to LF, so a checkout is byte-identical to what was
# committed no matter what the machine's autocrlf happens to be. Measured, not
# assumed: a checkout with autocrlf=true reported 65 of the 67 vendored files as
# changed, and every one of them was a CRLF difference.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ── binary ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.png binary
*.vsix binary
*.tgz binary
*.ico binary

# ── keep the vendored tree out of the language stats ───────────────────────
# It is somebody else's code, and without this GitHub reports this repository as
# mostly JavaScript that this project did not write.
hud/** linguist-vendored
node_modules/** linguist-vendored
media/react.js linguist-vendored
media/xterm.js linguist-vendored

# ── diffs worth reading ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The bundles and the vendored browser half are one long line each; a text diff of
# them is noise, and marking them generated keeps them out of the diff stat.
media/react.js -diff
media/xterm.js -diff
hud/client.js -diff